# Kernel messages a test provokes on purpose, one per line: the test,
# the text the message contains between double quotes, then why the
# test provokes it. A line that starts with # is a comment.
#
# beamfs-xfstests reads this file (2.5.4 and later). A message of the
# kernel at warn or above while a test runs fails it, even when
# xfstests passed it, unless this file names the test and the text.
# The tree checker's LOST POINTER and a correction by beamfs are never
# set aside, whatever this file says. A line without a text or without
# a reason is refused. No text names a device number, so that a line
# holds on every node.
#
# Seen on 0.1.26, Linux 7.3-rc6, x86-01: the sweep of the auto group
# sweep-1791696476 (30c1747, beamfs-xfstests 2.5.3) and the trial
# sweep-1791793040 (1dbbbf8, beamfs-xfstests 2.5.4) logged the same
# messages for these tests. The trial sweep-1791797340 (080d3fd) also
# logged, for generic/361, the count of the errors the block layer
# held back, which the two runs before did not reach.
#
# generic/067, mount_nonexistent_mnt, mounts the scratch device without
# a type: libblkid does not know beamfs, so mount tries each block
# filesystem of /proc/filesystems in turn (ext3, ext4, ext2, squashfs,
# msdos, iso9660), and each that finds nothing says so; squashfs says
# nothing. Its mount_wrong_fstype then mounts the device as ext4.
generic/067 "VFS: Can't find ext4 filesystem" ext3 and ext4 tried by the mount without a type, then the mount as ext4: ext4 finds none on a beamfs volume
generic/067 "can't find an ext2 filesystem on dev" ext2 tried by the mount without a type: it finds none on a beamfs volume
generic/067 "Can't find a valid FAT filesystem" msdos tried by the mount without a type: FAT finds none on a beamfs volume
generic/067 "bogus number of reserved sectors" msdos tried by the mount without a type: FAT reads the beamfs superblock as a boot sector and rejects it
generic/067 "ISOFS: Unable to identify CD-ROM format" iso9660 tried by the mount without a type: it finds none on a beamfs volume
generic/338 "Buffer I/O error on dev dm-" puts dm-error under the mounted volume: its reads fail on purpose
generic/338 "beamfs: volume failed in " beamfs answers the errors dm-error returns by failing the volume and refusing writes
generic/361 "critical space allocation error, dev loop" runs a loop device over a sparse image on the scratch volume, whose writes fail with ENOSPC once that volume is full: the I/O error the test wants
generic/361 "Buffer I/O error on dev loop" the writes beamfs makes through the buffer cache to that full loop device fail, and the buffer layer reports each one lost
generic/361 "buffer_io_error: " the buffer layer rate-limits "Buffer I/O error on dev loop" the same way and says, at warn, how many it held back; seen on compute01 (sweep-1792102990), slower than x86-01, hence enough of them to be held back
generic/361 "blk_print_req_error: " the block layer prints the errors of that loop device ten at a time; past that it says, at warn, how many it held back
generic/361 "beamfs: volume failed in " beamfs answers the write errors of the loop device by failing the volume and refusing writes
generic/361 "beamfs: umount: the superblock did not reach the medium" the write of the superblock at umount fails on that full loop device, and beamfs says so rather than claim a clean unmount; seen on the 2026-10-15 trial (sweep-1792072824, vfs.all 9afee012f44a), where the four messages above did not appear
generic/441 "Buffer I/O error on dev dm-" switches dm-error to errors under open files, then checks that fsync reports them
generic/484 "Buffer I/O error on dev dm-" flips the data device to dm-error, then checks that syncfs reports the error
generic/633 "with NULL argv: empty string added" vfstest executes a file with an empty argv; fs/exec.c adds an empty string and says so
